1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,000
 Morning everyone. Last time we defined Big-O. Today we use it on a real algorithm: binary search.

2
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:21,000
 The input has to be sorted. If it's not sorted, nothing I say today works.

3
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:46,000
 Think of the guessing game. Every guess cuts the candidates in half.

4
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:36,000
 Here's the pseudocode. Notice it's lo less than or equal to hi. If you write strictly less than, you miss the last element. That's the bug I see every year.

5
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:16,000
 Let's trace it for twenty-three. Round one, mid is index four, value sixteen, too small, so lo becomes five.

6
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:16,000
 Why is it correct? The loop invariant: if x is in the array, it's in A from lo to hi. You will be asked to state this invariant in the exam and explain why lo becomes mid plus one.

7
00:38:00,000 --> 00:38:06,000
 The worst case recurrence is C of n equals C of n over two plus one. That solves to floor log two n plus one.

8
00:44:20,000 --> 00:44:26,000
 For homework, find an unsorted array of length five where binary search returns minus one even though the key is there.
